Higher-order methods.

A higher-order method is one that takes a function as a parameter, returns a function, or both. And you've been calling them all module — filter, map, sort, for-each all accept a function. This section is about writing your own, which is how you build APIs that let callers plug in behaviour.

Start with taking a function, to parameterise the behaviour. The moment a method has a "the part that varies" hole, a functional parameter fills it. Instead of hard-coding what to do, you accept it. Take a filter method: it loops over the input, and for each element, if the caller's predicate — call it keep — tests true, it adds the element to the output. Then filter of users, u, arrow, u is active, keeps the active ones. Filter owns the loop; the caller owns the criterion. This is the Strategy pattern from module two — but where you once passed an object implementing an interface, you now just pass a lambda. Any "do X, but let the caller decide the details of X" API is a higher-order method — a retry, a measure, a transaction.

Consider a retry, as one small higher-order method. It's generic, taking a number of times, and a supplier for the action. It loops up to that many times: each pass, it tries to return the action's get; on a runtime exception, it remembers it and loops again; and if all attempts fail, it throws the last one. So retry of three, empty-parens, arrow, H-T-T-P get of a url, retries any supplier up to three times. The policy — retry three times — lives in the method; the work — the H-T-T-P get — is passed in. One generic method now retries anything.

A method can also produce a function, capturing its arguments — a closure — to configure the returned behaviour. An adder method, taking an int called by, returns n, arrow, n plus by. It captures by, giving you a configured function. So adder of ten returns a specialised function; add-ten dot apply of five gives fifteen. This is currying, or partial application — turning a two-argument operation into a one-argument function by fixing one argument. And it's how you'd fake a tri-function: a function returning a function, returning a result.

The most powerful shape takes a function and returns an enhanced one — wrapping behaviour, exactly like a decorator. A logged method, generic in T and R, takes a function and returns t, arrow, a lambda that prints the input, applies the original function, prints the output, and returns the result. So logged of String double-colon length gives you the same length function, now with logging. Memoization, timing, logging, caching — all of them are "take a function, return a wrapped function", added without ever touching the original.

So here's the takeaway. Higher-order methods are where functions-as-values pays off in your code, not just the library's. Accept a functional parameter to let callers inject behaviour — the modern Strategy pattern. Return a function to build configured, or decorated, behaviour. And this is precisely how the stream A-P-I is designed — a chain of higher-order methods, each taking a function — which is why module seven will feel like a natural extension of everything here. The closing section steps back to the judgement call: when this functional style genuinely helps, and when plain imperative code is clearer.
